    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I've been honing the chisel on my stones pretty regularly, so I thought it was sharp enough, but maybe I'm missing something about the angle. Is there a specific way the edge should be shaped for this kind of detail work? And does the moisture content of the walnut change how much it splinters during the paring process?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For fine joinery like dovetails, a very acute, precise angle is often crucial for clean cuts, so check your bevel angle constantly. Moisture content definitely plays a role, as very dry walnut can be more brittle and prone to splintering when forcefully cut. Try lightly dampening the wood only if your climate dictates, but usually, a well-sharp tool bypasses the need for moisture intervention.",
      "source": "target",
