    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I'll look into the bevel angle again. One more thing—do you think it could be the way the wood is behaving naturally? Sometimes it feels like even when I'm being careful, the wood just refuses to cooperate or seems to fight back against the tool. Is that just a common frustration with walnut or is there something else going on structurally?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Walnut is known for having some directional inconsistencies and being quite dense, so occasional resistance is part of the process. This resistance is often related to the figure or way the grain wraps around, which can create varying hardness across the piece. Maintaining consistent, light strokes and letting your chisel work *with* the direction of the strongest grain flow is key to overcoming this natural fight.",
      "source": "target",
